<Weblab2>
  <config><![CDATA[{
  "game_id": 74,
  "created_at": "2026-05-28T18:15:29.000Z",
  "level_num": "custom",
  "user_id": 574,
  "properties": {
    "require_edit_to_continue": true,
    "start_sources": {
      "folders": {
      },
      "files": {
        "eb156ec8-ec96-46c7-b63b-4f376e08c554": {
          "id": "eb156ec8-ec96-46c7-b63b-4f376e08c554",
          "name": "index.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <title>Drawing Circles</title>\n  <style>\n    body {\n      font-family: sans-serif;\n      display: flex;\n      flex-direction: column;\n      align-items: center;\n      margin-top: 20px;\n    }\n    canvas {\n      border: 2px solid black;\n      background-color: lightgray;\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>My First Circle!</h1>\n  <canvas id=\"myCanvas\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\"></canvas>\n\n  <script src=\"script.js\"></script>\n</body>\n</html>",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": true
        },
        "2f7e872c-ceff-424b-90b8-272b81ba0341": {
          "id": "2f7e872c-ceff-424b-90b8-272b81ba0341",
          "name": "script.js",
          "contents": "const canvas = document.getElementById(\"myCanvas\");\nconst ctx = canvas.getContext(\"2d\");\n\n// The canvas is 400 pixels wide and 300 pixels high.\n// (0,0) is the top-left corner.\n// X increases to the right, Y increases downwards.\n\n/**\n * Helper function to draw a filled circle.\n * @param {number} x The x-coordinate of the circle's center.\n * @param {number} y The y-coordinate of the circle's center.\n * @param {number} radius The radius of the circle.\n * @param {string} color The color to fill the circle with (e.g., \"red\", \"blue\").\n */\nfunction drawCircle(x, y, radius, color) {\n  ctx.beginPath(); // Start drawing a new path\n  // arc(centerX, centerY, radius, startAngle, endAngle)\n  // For a full circle, startAngle is 0 and endAngle is Math.PI * 2 (which is 360 degrees)\n  ctx.arc(x, y, radius, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n  ctx.fillStyle = color;\n  ctx.fill(); // Fill the circle with the set color\n}\n\n// Let's draw our first circle!\n// Parameters are: (center X, center Y, radius, color)\ndrawCircle(100, 150, 40, \"red\");\n\n// Challenge:\n// 1. Change the color of the circle.\n// 2. Change the x and y values to move the circle to a different spot.\n//    Can you move it to the center of the canvas?\n// 3. Change the radius to make the circle bigger or smaller.\n//    What happens if the radius is 0? Or really big?",
          "folderId": "0",
          "type": "starter",
          "active": false
        }
      },
      "openFiles": [
        "eb156ec8-ec96-46c7-b63b-4f376e08c554",
        "2f7e872c-ceff-424b-90b8-272b81ba0341"
      ]
    },
    "long_instructions": "Welcome back to your digital canvas! In the last level, you learned how to draw rectangles using `ctx.fillRect()` and how x and y coordinates determine where shapes appear.\r\n\r\nNow, let's explore how to draw a new shape: a circle! Open up your `script.js` file. You'll see we've provided a special helper function called `drawCircle()`. This function makes it super easy to draw circles on your canvas.\r\n\r\nTake a close look at the example line that uses `drawCircle(100, 150, 40, \"red\");`. These numbers tell your computer exactly how to draw the circle:\r\n*   The first number (100) is the **x-coordinate** of the circle's *center*.\r\n*   The second number (150) is the **y-coordinate** of the circle's *center*.\r\n*   The third number (40) is the **radius** of the circle (how big it is from the center to its edge).\r\n*   The fourth value (\"red\") is the **color** of the circle.\r\n\r\nYour challenge is to play with these numbers in `script.js`:\r\n1.  Change the color of the circle to your favorite color (e.g., 'blue', 'green', 'purple', 'orange').\r\n2.  Change the first two numbers in `drawCircle()` to move your circle to a different spot on the canvas. Can you move it to the very center? (Remember the canvas is 400x300 pixels!)\r\n3.  Change the third number (the radius) to make your circle bigger or smaller. What happens if the radius is very small or very large?",
    "generate_outline": "Introduce students to drawing circles using a function like `drawCircle(x, y, radius)`. Students will practice drawing a circle at a specific coordinate with a given radius."
  },
